course: dictionary
lesson: 16013
title: Vastly: very much, or very large
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Vastly means very much, or over a very large area or amount. It makes a comparison or description much stronger. «The new library is vastly bigger than the old one.» The word vastly tells you the library is far bigger, not just a bit bigger.
explain: Vastly does not give an exact number. If someone says «the two answers were vastly different», you know the difference is big, but the sentence does not say how many points or metres apart they are. Look for the clues around the word to see how strong the comparison is.
explain: You often see vastly before words like different, better, improved or outnumbered. «Our team was vastly outnumbered, but we never gave up.» Here vastly shows the other team had far more players, and the word makes the sentence stronger. Try swapping in very much: if the meaning still works, vastly is doing the same job.
fact: Vastly means very much, or over a very large area or amount.
fact: «Vastly» is an adverb, so it tells you how something is: how big, how different or how improved.
fact: Common phrases include «vastly different», «vastly better» and «vastly outnumbered».
fact: Vastly does not give an exact number. It shows that the difference is very large.
fact: A good check is to swap in «very much» or «by a huge amount» and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «The new library is vastly bigger than the old one.» What does vastly mean here? | Open only on Sundays | Slightly smaller | *Very much bigger | Painted blue
exercise: «Our two maths answers were vastly different, so one of us must be wrong.» What does vastly tell you? | The answers were written in pencil | Only a little different | *The difference is very large | Exactly the same
exercise: «Thousands of birds flew over a vastly wide area of the sea.» Which meaning fits vastly here? | Very slowly | *Over a very large area | Only just | Over a tiny corner
test: «The old phone was vastly slower than the new one.» Which word is the clue that the new phone is much faster? | The | phone | *vastly | one
test: Mia says vastly means exactly 100 times. Is she right? | *No, vastly does not give an exact number, only shows a very large difference | Yes, because vastly is a maths word | Yes, because vastly always means one hundred | No, vastly means very much, not an exact number
exam: Which sentence uses vastly to mean very much? | «The bus was late again.» | «We walked to school this morning.» | *«Our garden is vastly improved since the spring.» | «The cat sat on the mat.»
